WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
- She's an Oscar winning
actress and an insanely

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.890
successful businesswoman
with her brand, Goop.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.880
Let's say hello to Gwyneth
Paltrow, everybody!

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.141
(upbeat music)
(applause and cheers)

00:00:11.141 --> 00:00:12.459
- Hi.
- Hello!

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:13.960
- How are you?

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:14.793
- I'm so good.

00:00:14.793 --> 00:00:16.760
Okay, I'm a little bummed
you took your glasses off.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.300
Those were so rock and roll.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.870
You had the black glasses.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:21.339
It looks so cool.

00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:22.440
- Can I leave them on?

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.181
I can see you better if I actually

00:00:24.181 --> 00:00:25.738
have them on.
- Okay, I'm just saying,

00:00:25.738 --> 00:00:27.040
you look cool in them.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.250
I would wear them if I
looked that good in them.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.280
But hey, we just spoke with a
woman who's headed to space.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:32.870
Would you ever go to space?

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.939
- Literally never.

00:00:34.939 --> 00:00:36.170
(laughter)

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.460
- I don't know that I would either.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.070
I find the thought exhilarating,

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.799
but then the actuality, like
it sets in and I'm like, mm.

00:00:42.799 --> 00:00:44.970
I don't know if it's 'cause we have kids.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.580
Do you think we would
have thought differently

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.520
if we were like 21 and no kids?

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.210
- I mean, I'm like afraid
to fly to Cleveland

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.443
on a regular plane.

00:00:52.443 --> 00:00:54.240
(laughter)

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.283
No way.
- So not a flyer.

00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:57.980
Well, how about your daughter?

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.120
She's 16 now, is she adventurous?

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.680
- She's incredibly adventurous.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.579
I mean, it's interesting.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:06.970
She's like a great mix of
pragmatic and adventurous.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:11.020
She loves to kind of explore life

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.061
outside her comfort zone

00:01:13.061 --> 00:01:15.339
but then she's very
logical at the same time.

00:01:15.339 --> 00:01:16.570
I think it's a good combo.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.020
- Yeah, it sounds very balanced.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.310
I'm gonna work on that.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.141
16 year old seems to be nailing it.

00:01:21.141 --> 00:01:24.821
Speaking of kids, I saw
this photo that you posted,

00:01:24.821 --> 00:01:27.579
you said, "Little dude in the back

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.237
pretending he didn't eat half my sando."

00:01:30.237 --> 00:01:32.280
They don't ever ask!

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.181
My kids just walk up and take my stuff.

00:01:34.181 --> 00:01:36.060
Does that happen all the time to you?

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:36.893
- Yes.

00:01:36.893 --> 00:01:37.726
You know, it's so funny.

00:01:37.726 --> 00:01:39.480
I remember actually before I had kids

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.181
I have a cousin, first cousin
who I'm really close to,

00:01:42.181 --> 00:01:45.520
her name's Lecy and I
remember her telling me,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.850
she's like, "Having kids is
the best thing in the world,

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.550
but it's a totally different game."

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.730
She's like, "I mean, you make
yourself a whole sandwich,

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:55.700
and then they just take it
and you don't even care,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.070
you just give them the sandwich."

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.060
But I've always remembered that.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.063
So, yeah.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.250
I think it's good when kids feel

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.870
that level of healthy
entitlement for a parent

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.670
and like what you're supposed
to provide for them, you know?

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:11.503
- Yeah.

00:02:11.503 --> 00:02:13.310
Well I think a healthy
dose of entitlement,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:14.680
not too much.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:15.913
- [Gwyneth] Yeah, true.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.880
- Do your kids like to tease you?

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.750
- Oh my God, constantly, constantly.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.550
I mean, we're out of it now,

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.270
but I went through a
phase with my daughter

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:31.040
where I wasn't allowed
to like hum, dance, move.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.323
Like I was mortifying her on every level.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.170
She makes fun of me more than my son does.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.822
My son, he's like,

00:02:39.822 --> 00:02:44.000
his humor is different,
but she just like, yeah,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.600
she's always making fun of me.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.350
- Oh my, my daughter's already doing it

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.279
at the age of six.

00:02:49.279 --> 00:02:50.470
It's been great foreshadowing

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.760
for what's to come.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.380
But I tweeted you a while back

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.310
and I said that you have an honest

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.290
and captivating voice and I
asked you to make an album.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.600
So, Gwyneth, how's the album coming along?

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:03.510
- Oh my God.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.223
This was so nice of you, by the way.

00:03:05.223 --> 00:03:06.580
- I meant it, by the way!

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.130
I think that you are a great singer,

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.420
like in different movies,
I've seen you singing.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.820
Do you love singing?

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:12.850
- I do.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.370
I find it really...

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.610
I find it hard.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:20.830
Like I think there's a
vulnerability that comes

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:21.760
out when you sing.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.520
That's like, for me, it's a bit shocking

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.125
and I think that's a good thing.

00:03:26.125 --> 00:03:29.450
I like being a backup singer more

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.640
like I love singing harmonies
and stuff like that.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.020
There's no way I could make
an album or do what you do.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.250
I feel like I've been able
to hide behind characters

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.240
who sing and things,
but if it was just me,

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.981
it would be way too vulnerable, no way.

00:03:43.981 --> 00:03:44.814
- I just liked it.

00:03:44.814 --> 00:03:48.200
I appreciate any singer
that it sounds vulnerable

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.510
like what you said, and
it sounds believable

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:52.141
but you're an actor.

00:03:52.141 --> 00:03:53.480
So I guess when you play
the characters, it's easier,

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.210
like you said, to dip into
those feelings or whatever

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.060
but that's what I loved about your voice

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.261
and in the few things
I've seen you sing in.

00:03:59.261 --> 00:04:00.820
So this is hilarious to me.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.388
You were patient zero in "Contagion".

00:04:03.388 --> 00:04:06.200
Did you think that
would ever really happen

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.690
in our lifetimes?

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:09.430
- Oh my gosh.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:10.723
Can you believe it?

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.530
- No, actually still, no.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.080
- It's just nuts.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:17.900
You know, it's funny, when
we were making the movie

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:22.240
and we were on the set,
I remember the producer

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.890
and writer saying, "No,
this is gonna happen."

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:27.351
I mean, 'cause the writer
had been researching

00:04:27.351 --> 00:04:30.087
these kinds of viruses and he's like,

00:04:30.087 --> 00:04:32.950
"We're due for one, it's
gonna happen in our lifetime."

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:34.650
And I thought, I can't
even think about that.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:36.860
That's just way too scary to even fathom.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.440
And I can't believe that
it actually has happened.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.350
I mean, we've all just been through it.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:42.650
It's been wild.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:43.790
- I know it's a crazy thing too,

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:45.810
when you have kids to try and explain

00:04:46.978 --> 00:04:49.670
what's happening when you
yourself are kind of lost.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.940
But you chose, this actually
I've found interesting as well,

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.220
you chose to not take lead
roles in movies anymore.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.540
So what was the reason for that?

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.610
'Cause you're awesome, so
it seems kind of a waste

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:00.700
for all of us.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:03.940
- Aw, you're so nice.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:06.823
Well, my kids, I mean, I just,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.320
you know, I just couldn't bear getting

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.380
on a plane and being away for months

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.963
at a time or missing bedtime.

00:05:20.114 --> 00:05:22.550
I just really wanted to be home as much

00:05:23.538 --> 00:05:25.378
as possible while they're
still in the house.

00:05:25.378 --> 00:05:28.080
You know, it's a finite
time and it's hit me so much

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.780
my daughter is now a
junior in high school.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:31.010
It's gone so fast

00:05:32.338 --> 00:05:35.820
and in a year and a half,
she'll be in college.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.310
And of course, hopefully
we'll maintain our closeness

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.060
in our relationship, but that just,

00:05:42.004 --> 00:05:43.517
that her life begins.

00:05:43.517 --> 00:05:44.679
And you know what I mean?

00:05:44.679 --> 00:05:45.680
It's such a finite time that they're home.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.410
So I wanted to try to focus
more on work that I could do

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:52.880
at home or at an office around the corner

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.590
and not so much traveling
or those, you know, big 16,

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.990
18 hour days that when
you're shooting something,

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:02.600
you're just out of the
house for that long.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.436
- Yeah, no, it is hard
definitely finding that balance

00:06:05.436 --> 00:06:09.590
with work and personal life.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:12.330
Well, Steven Spielberg is your godfather,

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:13.973
so that's random.

00:06:14.859 --> 00:06:17.740
Your life is crazy.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:20.440
Has he documented any moments of your life

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.379
and do they look epic?

00:06:22.379 --> 00:06:25.280
(laughter)

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:26.630
- That is so funny.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:31.630
You know, he is, he always has
a video camera in his hands.

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:36.540
So any family event, 4th
of July, Thanksgiving

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.330
whatever the case may
be, he's always videoing.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:43.930
So yes, he definitely has videoed me

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:46.238
over the years and he was actually

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:48.950
the only person who was videoing

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:51.140
at my wedding two and a half years ago.

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:54.410
So I guess I had a
pretty good videographer.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:57.130
- You had a great wedding videographer.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.156
(laughter)

00:06:58.156 --> 00:07:00.060
That's amazing.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:01.033
Did he charge you?

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:04.080
That's the best part.

00:07:05.355 --> 00:07:06.513
- He knew-

00:07:06.513 --> 00:07:08.410
- That's amazing though, you've
been married for two years.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:11.229
I mean, coming from
someone who's literally

00:07:11.229 --> 00:07:12.070
in the midst of divorce,

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:15.090
I can't even imagine doing it again.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:16.510
So, I mean, that's amazing

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:18.470
that you found love and
you found this space

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.380
and the vulnerability, that level

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:22.390
to say yes and do it again.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:23.970
Was that hard for you?

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:27.940
- Probably the hardest
thing I've ever done

00:07:29.132 --> 00:07:31.930
and probably allowing
myself to learn the lessons

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:35.160
that I needed to learn from my divorce

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.260
and then being able to
really kind of do that

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:43.960
work on myself and then
open myself up again.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:47.270
And my husband, he just,
he requires that kind

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.900
of presence and intimacy, and
it was really hard for me.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.860
Like I wasn't good at it before.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.095
I still struggle with it, but
you will have it again, Kelly,

00:07:57.095 --> 00:07:59.570
it just takes time.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:02.360
- Well, I'm actually in that
place where I think a lot

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.350
of people I've heard
that go through divorce.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.390
It's almost like you start
dating yourself again.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:10.570
Like you actually make time for you again.

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:13.213
And I love dating me.

00:08:15.036 --> 00:08:17.467
So I'm like, I'm actually
not looking for it,

00:08:17.467 --> 00:08:19.960
but it's also a beautiful
thing for a lot of people

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.900
like me that are going through
divorce that do want that

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.780
in the future to let know
that it can, you know,

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:27.212
and will probably happen for you.

00:08:27.212 --> 00:08:30.830
But how did you learn co-parenting

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:31.663
with your ex?

00:08:31.663 --> 00:08:33.610
Because co-parenting
can be very difficult,

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:37.700
because I think divorce is hard
on people, but it's hardest

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.930
on the kids and I'm from
a divorced family too,

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:42.046
twice over as a kid.

00:08:42.046 --> 00:08:43.430
So it's harder on the kids.

00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:44.340
Was that all right with you?

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:46.360
Is that easier or is it hard to navigate?

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:51.360
- I think we made a commitment
early on to be a family

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.163
even though we weren't a couple,

00:08:56.175 --> 00:08:59.070
which, you know, sounds
like weird and impossible,

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:02.910
but we worked with a
therapist who was amazing

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:05.113
at helping us communicate.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:10.140
And we, luckily we both came

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.690
from families that were
strong and, you know,

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:14.897
our parents were still together.

00:09:14.897 --> 00:09:17.135
My parents were together
until my father passed away.

00:09:17.135 --> 00:09:20.340
We really did want the same things.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.520
And we wanted the divorce ideally to

00:09:23.642 --> 00:09:25.996
impact the kids as little as possible.

00:09:25.996 --> 00:09:28.157
And I had talked to a lot of
people, children of divorce

00:09:28.157 --> 00:09:31.510
and I said, you know, what can
I learn from your experience?

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:35.300
And everybody had the same
thing, which was just,

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.360
I wanted my parents to be
friends and like each other.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:42.350
And I didn't want to feel
that terrible feeling

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:44.590
when my dad wouldn't come into the house

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:48.223
or my mom made my dad drop
us at the end of the road.

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:53.750
So we just tried to make a
commitment to really stay close

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:56.163
and we've been able to do that.

00:09:57.527 --> 00:09:58.670
It wasn't easy.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:03.670
I'm proud of us for having
kind of really stuck to it.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:06.670
And now it's many years.

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.920
And so it's much easier now.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:12.313
It's harder in the beginning
of course, but you just have to

00:10:12.313 --> 00:10:15.857
make a concerted effort
to really think about

00:10:15.857 --> 00:10:19.095
what is best for the
children in this moment

00:10:19.095 --> 00:10:20.570
and then you just have
to kind of push your own

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:23.230
reactivity down as best you can.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:25.180
- [Kelly] Absolutely, yeah, absolutely.

